-- Binary sums: A + B is a tagged union — an ordinary inductive (the prelude's
-- `Sum`, with `+` as notation). It is built with the qualified constructors
-- Sum.inl / Sum.inr and consumed with its recursor Sum.rec: a motive saying
-- what to conclude about the scrutinee, then one branch per injection. Sums are
-- positive: there is no η, and a stuck sum is used only by recursion.
--
-- Universe-polymorphic: `Sum` lands at the max of its summands' levels, so on
-- two `Prop`s it is itself a `Prop` — a genuine proof-irrelevant disjunction
-- (see the `Or` section at the bottom).

axiom A : Type
axiom B : Type

-- the non-dependent eliminator (Lean's Sum.elim): the constant motive is
-- written here once. A checked constructor application may drop the type
-- parameters (the elaborator recovers them), but a recursor takes them
-- explicitly.
def elim (X Y Z : Type) (f : X → Z) (g : Y → Z) (s : X + Y) : Z :=
  Sum.rec X Y (λ x : X + Y ⇒ Z) f g s

def swap (X Y : Type) (s : X + Y) : Y + X :=
  elim X Y (Y + X) (λ x : X ⇒ (Sum.inr x : Y + X))
    (λ y : Y ⇒ (Sum.inl y : Y + X)) s

-- ι-reduction: the recursor picks the matching branch
axiom a : A
axiom b : B
#check_equal (swap A B (Sum.inl a)) (Sum.inr a)
#check_equal (swap A B (Sum.inr b)) (Sum.inl b)
-- double swap is the identity on injections...
#check_equal (swap B A (swap A B (Sum.inl a))) (Sum.inl a)
-- ...but NOT definitionally on stuck values (no η for sums): on a stuck s, both
-- sides freeze as distinct neutral recursions.

-- A ∨ B as a Prop. Because Sum is universe-polymorphic, applied to two
-- propositions it forms in Prop (its level is max 0 0 = 0): a logical
-- disjunction, not just a tagged union of data. The injections and Sum.rec are
-- exactly the same machinery as above; only the level instantiation differs.
def Or (P Q : Prop) : Prop := P + Q
def Or_inl (P Q : Prop) (x : P) : Or P Q := Sum.inl P Q x
theorem Or_comm (P Q : Prop) (h : Or P Q) : Or Q P :=
  Sum.rec P Q (λ _ : P + Q ⇒ Or Q P) (λ x : P ⇒ Sum.inr Q P x)
    (λ y : Q ⇒ Sum.inl Q P y) h
